---
/**
 * The four portfolios side by side, a short test for which one is yours, and the ladder
 * that sets how much of the trend fund to hold.
 *
 * The comparison table types its cells because a table is where a reader scans; the
 * measured numbers in the prose beneath render from figure records.
 */
import AllocationBar from "~/components/AllocationBar.astro";
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Ladder from "~/components/Ladder.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import { PORTFOLIOS } from "~/content/portfolios";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const holdingsText = (p: (typeof PORTFOLIOS)[number]) => p.holdings.map((h) => `${h.ticker} ${h.weight}%`).join(", ");

const ROWS = [
  { label: "Holdings", cells: PORTFOLIOS.map(holdingsText) },
  { label: "Fee a year", cells: PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => p.fee) },
  { label: "Fee on $10,000", cells: PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => p.feeOn10k) },
  {
    label: "What it adds over the one before",
    cells: [
      "Nothing to add. This is the market.",
      "Fees and tax: 0.04 to 2.70 points a year, depending on what you hold now",
      "About 0.8 points a year over a cheap index mix, 1990 to 2026",
      "About 2.0 points a year over an all-stock fund, 1929 to 2025, fading to 0.4 since 2009",
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Worst stretch",
    cells: [
      "Fell 52.7% and took 63 months to recover, 1990 to 2026",
      "Same as portfolio one",
      "Cheap US stocks 54.3% behind the market over 17.7 years, still not recovered",
      "Fell 50.3% and took 42 months to recover, 1990 to 2026, with RSST at 25%",
    ],
  },
] as const;

const LADDER = [
  { worstFall: "−30%", rsst: "11%" },
  { worstFall: "−40%", rsst: "15%", extra: "Plus 10 points in inflation-protected Treasuries (TIPS)" },
  { worstFall: "−50%", rsst: "19%", extra: "Plus 10 points in TIPS" },
  { worstFall: "−60%", rsst: "24%" },
  { worstFall: "About −70%", rsst: "30%", published: true },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="The four portfolios, side by side"
  wide
  description="Holdings, fees, what each portfolio adds, how far each has fallen, and a short test for which one is yours."
>
  <Hero
    title="Four portfolios, side by side"
    lede="Each one contains the one before it. The question is not which is best. It is how much of the clever stuff you can hold through a bad decade."
  />

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-8" heading="The comparison" maxWidth="64rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[58rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">The four portfolios compared on holdings, fees, what each adds, worst stretch and confidence</caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left align-bottom">
          <td role="cell" class="py-2 pr-4"></td>
          {
            PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4">
                <a href={p.href} class="link">{`${p.number}. ${p.name}`}</a>
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-2">
        {
          ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule align-top">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-medium text-ink">
                {row.label}
              </th>
              {row.cells.map((cell, i) => (
                <td role="cell" data-label={`${PORTFOLIOS[i]?.number}. ${PORTFOLIOS[i]?.name}`} class="py-2 pr-4">
                  {cell}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
        <tr role="row" class="align-top">
          <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-medium text-ink">How sure I am</th>
          {
            PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => (
              <td role="cell" data-label={`${p.number}. ${p.name}`} class="py-2 pr-4">
                <Verdict word={p.confidence} />
              </td>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-5">
    {
      PORTFOLIOS.map((p) => (
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-1">{`${p.number}. ${p.name}`}</p>
          <AllocationBar holdings={p.holdings} name={`${p.number}. ${p.name}`} />
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-8">
    <p>The "what it adds" row compares each portfolio with a different thing, on purpose. Portfolio two is compared
    with whatever you hold today, because that is where its saving comes from. Portfolios three and four are
    compared with a cheap index mix holding the same split of US and international stocks. The three figures do
    not add up to one number, and I have not added them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What each is for</h2>

    <p><strong>One fund</strong> is the market. Over ten years to mid-2025, 90.3% of US stock funds run by
    professionals did worse than it. If you hold nothing else and never sell, you will do better than most of
    them.</p>

    <p><strong>Held well</strong> is the same fund with four habits: the cheapest version of it, the right account
    for each piece, an ETF rather than a mutual fund, and no trading. The habits are worth between 0.04 and 2.70
    points a year. The low end is someone already in cheap index funds. The high end is someone leaving an
    expensive fund in a taxable account. Everything here is arithmetic, not a forecast.</p>

    <p><strong>Value lean</strong> replaces the one fund with six, and leans toward companies that are cheaper,
    smaller and more profitable than average. Over 1990 to 2026 that lean earned about
    <Figure id="value-lean-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> a year over a cheap index mix, with a range of 0.4 to
    1.3. It is a bet with a long record and a long losing stretch inside it.</p>

    <p><strong>With trend</strong> adds one fund, RSST, that holds US stocks and a trend-following program at the
    same time. It is the only holding on this site that adds an idea on top of stocks rather than swapping stocks
    for it. On 96 years of data it added about <Figure id="trend-96-year-gap" inline /> over an all-stock
    fund. Since 2009 the gap has been <Figure id="trend-gap-since-2009" inline showUnit={false} />,
    which is too small to tell from luck.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Which one is yours</h2>

    <p>Answer one question: how far could your portfolio fall before you sold?</p>

    <ul>
      <li>If the answer is less than 30%, none of these as written. Hold fewer stocks and more bonds.</li>
      <li>If it is 30% to 50%, hold portfolio two with some bonds beside it, or portfolio four at the row of the
      ladder below that matches your number. Every portfolio on this page fell by about half at some point since
      1990.</li>
      <li>If it is 50% or more and you would rather match the market than risk trailing it for a decade, hold
      portfolio two.</li>
      <li>If it is 50% or more and you want a lean with a long record, hold portfolio three.</li>
      <li>If it is 50% or more and you want a real chance of beating the market, hold portfolio four, with the RSST
      share the ladder gives for your number.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The ladder</h2>

    <p>The share of portfolio four in RSST is set by the worst fall you would sit through. The published 30% assumes
    a tolerance near 70%, which is more than most people mean when they say they can hold on.</p>
  </div>

  <Ladder rows={LADDER} class="my-6" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>There is one more row that is not on the ladder. Someone who looks at today's stock prices and wants to act on
    that concern, without cutting stocks outright, can hold RSST at 25% and put 5 points into ten-year TIPS. That
    version sits between the −50% and −60% rows. Over the past 96 years it would have trailed the 30% version by
    about <Figure id="working-default-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, and at today's more modest expected
    returns by 0 to 0.2 points, in exchange for a worst fall 1.8 points shallower. The
    <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">with-trend page</a> explains the choice.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The decision that is bigger than all of this</h2>

    <p>How much of your money is in stocks at all matters more than anything on this page. Moving from 60% stocks to
    90% stocks added about <Figure id="equity-share-step" inline showUnit={false} /> a year over 1963 to 2025, and
    it takes about <Figure id="equity-step-horizon" inline /> of results to be confident that gap was not luck.
    Nothing above changes that decision. Set your stock share from the fall you can sit through, and then pick a
    portfolio.</p>

    <p>Every figure on this page is before tax and after fund costs. What the numbers are worth to you depends on
    your accounts and on what you hold today, and no page here assumes either.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
